@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Align Textures with the UV Editor") {
        > This page is a continuation of <doc:04.modeler_paint>. Please open your
        > `courtyard.tscn` scene.

        In the reference, see <doc:/documentation/Xogot/Xogot-Modeler#UV-Editor>.

        UVs tell the renderer which part of a texture goes on each part of a face. In
        this tutorial, you use the UV Editor to set the texture size on the floor, to
        line up the texture across the walls, and to fit an image on the sign.

        @Image(source: "modeler-05-intro-uvs.png",
               alt: "The courtyard with aligned stone textures on the walls and floor")
    }

    @Section(title: "Open the UV Editor") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The UV Editor opens in the **Mesh UV** tab of the bottom panel. It shows the
            UVs of the selected mesh on a flat canvas.

            @Image(source: "modeler-05-mesh-uv-panel.png",
                   alt: "The Mesh UV bottom panel with the toolbar, canvas, control groups, and status bar")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Select the floor. Select **UV Editor** in the Object Actions menu.

                You can also use **UV Editor** in the Editable Mesh inspector, or the
                **Mesh UV** tab. The tab is available only while an editable mesh is
                selected.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-open-uv-editor.png",
                       alt: "The UV Editor command in the Object Actions menu")
            }

            @Step {
                Look at the toolbar.

                From left to right: the **Select**, **Move**, **Rotate**, and **Scale**
                tools, the **Vertex**, **Edge**, and **Face** modes, **Channel**,
                **Scope**, **Texture**, and **Center UV View**.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-toolbar.png",
                       alt: "The UV Editor toolbar")
            }

            @Step {
                Select **Face** in the toolbar, and click or tap the floor face on the
                canvas.

                The same face is selected in the viewport. The status bar shows `1
                selected`. Blue faces are Auto. Orange faces are Manual.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-selection-sync.png",
                       alt: "The same face selected in the viewport and on the UV canvas, with the status bar")
            }

            @Step {
                Make sure that **Texture** is on.

                The canvas shows the texture of the selected material behind the UVs.
                The square outline is the 0–1 texture area.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-texture-preview.png",
                       alt: "The Texture toggle on, with the floor texture behind the UVs")
            }

            @Step {
                Pinch to zoom the canvas. Drag with two fingers, or scroll on a trackpad,
                to pan.

                Press 0, or select **Center UV View**, to frame the selection again.

            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Tile the Floor") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            New shapes use Auto UVs. Xogot calculates them from the face and its
            settings, so the texture stays correct when you edit the mesh.

            @Image(source: "modeler-05-auto-tiling.png",
                   alt: "The floor texture tiled with Auto UVs")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                With the floor face selected, find the **Auto UVs** group. Make sure that
                **Fill** is **Tile**.

                With **Tile**, one world unit is one texture repeat, so a 20-unit floor
                shows the texture 20 times.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-auto-group.png",
                       alt: "The Auto UVs group with Fill set to Tile")
            }

            @Step {
                Set **Tiling U** and **Tiling V** to 0.25, and select **Apply Auto
                Settings**.

                A smaller tiling value makes the texture larger. Now each texture repeat
                is four units wide.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-tiling-values.png",
                       alt: "Tiling U and Tiling V set to 0.25, with Apply Auto Settings")
            }

            @Step {
                Set **Rotation** to 45, and select **Apply Auto Settings**. Then set it
                back to 0, and apply again.

                Use **Offset U**, **Offset V**, and **Anchor** to move the texture on the
                face. **Tiling Presets** sets both tiling values at once.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-rotation.png",
                       alt: "The floor texture rotated 45 degrees")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Line Up the Walls") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Separate objects can share one world-space texture, so that the stone rows
            line up at the corners.

            @Image(source: "modeler-05-walls-aligned.png",
                   alt: "Stone rows that line up at the corner of two walls")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Select a wall. In the viewport, select its inner face.

                The UV Editor now shows the wall.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-wall-face.png",
                       alt: "The inner face of a wall selected in the viewport and in the UV Editor")
            }

            @Step {
                Turn on **World Space**, and select **Apply Auto Settings**.

                The texture now uses world positions. It stays in place when you move the
                wall. Do the same on the other walls.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-world-space.png",
                       alt: "World Space turned on in the Auto UVs group")
            }

            @Step {
                Select the stairs. In **Face** mode, select the top faces of all steps and
                the front faces between them.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-stair-faces.png",
                       alt: "The top and front faces of the stairs selected")
            }

            @Step {
                Select **New Group** in the **Auto UVs** group.

                The faces are now in one texture group. Xogot projects them together, so
                the texture continues across the steps. The faces in a group must be
                edge-adjacent.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-texture-group.png",
                       alt: "The stair faces in one texture group with a continuous texture")
            }

            To add more faces to the group, type its number in **Texture Group**, and
            select **Join**. **Break** removes faces from their group. **Select Group**
            selects all the faces of a group.
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Fit an Image on the Sign") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Manual UVs keep the values that you set. Use them to put a complete image on
            a face.

            @Image(source: "modeler-05-manual-uvs.png",
                   alt: "The sign with a manual UV layout that shows the complete image")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Paint the Sprite sign with a material that has a sign image. Select the
                sign face.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-sign-selected.png",
                       alt: "The sign face selected, with its image on the canvas")
            }

            @Step {
                In the **Manual UVs** group, select **Planar**.

                Xogot projects the face flat, and changes it to Manual. The face now shows
                in orange on the canvas.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-planar-projection.png",
                       alt: "Planar projection applied to the sign, shown in orange")
            }

            @Step {
                Select **Island** in **Selection and Grid**, and then select **Fit**.

                **Fit** scales the UVs to fill the 0–1 texture area, so the complete
                image shows on the sign.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-fit-uvs.png",
                       alt: "The sign UV island fitted to the texture area")
            }

            @Step {
                If the image is mirrored, select **Flip Horizontal** or **Flip Vertical**.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-flip.png",
                       alt: "The sign image flipped to read correctly")
            }

            To go back to calculated UVs, select **Convert to Auto**. This replaces
            your manual UVs. Use Undo if you change your mind.
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Adjust UVs by Hand") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The canvas tools move, rotate, and scale the selected UVs with a drag.

            @Image(source: "modeler-05-canvas-tools.png",
                   alt: "The Rotate tool on the canvas with the pivot marker")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Select the flower bed, and select its top face in **Face** mode. Select
                the **Rotate** tool.

                A pivot marker, a cross in a circle, shows at the center of the
                selection.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-rotate-tool.png",
                       alt: "The Rotate tool selected with the pivot marker at the face center")
            }

            @Step {
                Hold Command or Ctrl, and drag on the canvas to turn the texture by 45°.

                The rotation snaps to steps of 15°. On a touch screen, turn on **Snap to
                Grid** in **Selection and Grid**.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-rotate-drag.gif",
                       alt: "The texture rotated by dragging on the canvas")
            }

            @Step {
                Drag the pivot marker to a corner of the face, and then scale with the
                **Scale** tool.

                The texture now scales from that corner. **Center UV View** puts the pivot
                back at the center.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-pivot-drag.png",
                       alt: "The pivot marker moved to a corner of the face")
            }

            @Step {
                To type exact values, use the **Transform** group. Set **Move U**,
                **Move V**, **Rotation**, **Scale U**, or **Scale V**, and select **Apply
                Transform**.

                In **Face** mode, Auto faces stay Auto. In Vertex and Edge modes, the
                faces change to Manual.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-transform-group.png",
                       alt: "The Transform group with Apply Transform")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Stitch Two Faces") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            **Stitch** joins the UVs of two adjacent faces along their common edge. This
            removes a seam in the texture.

            @Image(source: "modeler-05-stitch.png",
                   alt: "Two platform faces stitched so the texture continues over the edge")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Select the platform. In **Face** mode, select its top face and one
                adjacent side face.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-stitch-select.png",
                       alt: "The top face and one side face of the platform selected")
            }

            @Step {
                Select **Stitch** in the **Manual UVs** group.

                The side face moves and turns on the canvas to line up with the top face.
                The texture now continues over the edge.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-stitch-result.png",
                       alt: "The stitched faces side by side on the UV canvas")
            }

            On macOS, you can also select one face, and then Command-click an adjacent
            face on the canvas to stitch it. Command-Shift-click copies the layout of the
            selected face to the clicked face.
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Move a Texture in the Viewport") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            **Scope** lets the viewport handles change UVs instead of geometry. You can
            align a texture while you look at the 3D scene.

            @Image(source: "modeler-05-uv-scope.png",
                   alt: "Scope turned on, with the viewport move handle shifting the texture on a wall")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Turn on **Scope** in the UV Editor toolbar. Select a wall face, and drag
                the move handle in the viewport.

                The texture moves on the face. The geometry does not move.

            }

            @Step {
                Turn off **Scope** to edit geometry again.

                Shift-drag does not extrude while **Scope** is on.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-uv-scope-off.png",
                       alt: "The Scope toggle turned off")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Check Lightmap UVs and Save a UV Image") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Baked lighting uses a second UV channel, UV2. A UV image helps you paint your
            own texture.

            @Image(source: "modeler-05-uv2.png",
                   alt: "The UV2 channel with the Lightmap UVs group")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Select the back wall. In the Editable Mesh inspector, select **Generate
                Lightmap UVs**.

                Xogot writes UV2. Your UV channel and your geometry do not change.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-generate-lightmap.png",
                       alt: "The Generate Lightmap UVs command in the inspector")
            }

            @Step {
                Set **Channel** to **UV2** in the toolbar.

                The canvas shows the lightmap layout. UV2 is read-only. Select
                **Rebuild Selected UV2** in the **Lightmap UVs** group to generate it
                again.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-uv2-channel.png",
                       alt: "The Channel menu set to UV2, with the Lightmap UVs group")
            }

            @Step {
                Set **Channel** back to **UV**. Select the sign.

                In the **UV Template** group, set **Image Size** to 1024.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-template-group.png",
                       alt: "The UV Template group with Image Size, Hide Grid, and the color settings")
            }

            @Step {
                Select **Save UV Image**, and save the file.

                Open the PNG in an image editor, and paint your texture over the lines.

                @Image(source: "modeler-05-save-template.png",
                       alt: "A saved UV image of the sign")
            }

            Save the scene. In the next tutorial, you add collision and a trigger.
        }
    }
}
